A||N||11||bad88||Cooling system question||||9||
Z||000000||bad88||05-03-2000||09:49 PM||||My question is this:  I had my 88 over heat a few days ago and I swapped out the heater hose from the intake manifold to the firewall thinking that was the problem.  after swapping that out (not made anymore but standard hose works o.k.) the car overheated much more quickly so I figured the pump because I wasn't getting any indication of flow at the radiator or the pressure cap even with the thermostat removed.  Swapped the pump (not alot of fun but not too bad) and the system still overheated so I filled it up and it seemed to be better but I sprung a leak between the engine and firewall.  There are no hoses back there acording to the manual, only a pipe (#2 in the service manual).  I'm thinking that maybe the pipe got cracked somehow with the proximity of the exhaust manifold.  After the long winded story here's the question....Has anyone successfully taken that pipe out without removing the engine?  If so how?  Let me know so I can get my baby back on the road again, otherwise it goes to the shop to get fixed when I can afford it. Thanks in advance||||reg||9
Z||000001||TOM||05-03-2000||10:31 PM||||I had to replace a coolant pipe once after a tire jockey used it for a jack point. Duh I thouht that was a frame rail. I got the tire for free once I told him what was involved to fix the crushed pipe. I cut the bad pipe someplace near the center and cut a good pipe from a junker and used a piece of radiator hose and two clamps to make the joint. I dont think the problem with removing the pipes is the engine it is getting the car high enough to get it out from around the front suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||05-04-2000||01:35 AM||||Back to the top...<P>I'm the one who helped Bad88 with the water pump, and I was kinda bummed that it didn't fix it.  Thanks for the help Tom, any other ideas?<P>And if you're reading this Brian, I'm gonna talk to Brad(the fiero guy) today and see what he has the part or would be willing to fix it. ||||reg||4
Z||000003||fiero56||05-04-2000||08:51 AM||||Sounds like you have the v-6. I have an '88 v-6, and have sucessfully installed the 180 degree 'stat before. It just popped right it. Sounds like you might have a little corrosion in the filler neck preventing the 'stat from fully seating. <P>My next action would be to check the radiator for blockage.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 05-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-04-2000||10:40 AM||||It's quite possible that you never got all the air burped out. It's a pain sometimes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bad88||05-04-2000||11:16 AM||||after reading the posts I think you guys are confused about the pipe I'm reffering to.  It's not the ones that run along the bottom of the car but the one that attaches from that pipe on the driver side to the thermostat hose.  The service manual calls it pipe #2 and its actually mounted to the engine by the foreward exhaust manifold....that's where the leak is.  Anyway I brought it down to the shop and they said they could rig something to work.  I hate doing that because everything (except the stereo) is stock on the car.  But I guess if Pontiac doesn't support it anymore we have to do something to keep the cars running.  I'll keep this topic posted on how it goes||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||05-04-2000||12:05 PM||||Bad... This pipe is commonly referred to as a coolant crossover pipe. It's supposed to be the only non-stainless coolant pipe on the car. Rodney Dickman has reproduced these in stainless.  <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/coolanttube.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/coolanttube.html</A> <BR>It's a very nice piece, as is everything that Rodney sells. GM no longer sells this part for an 88.<BR>It IS a real *pain-in-the-ass* to replace with the engine in the car, but it can be done. <BR>Drop the top AC bracket, the starter, the oil filter, and the heat shield (if there is one) from the cat. There are some ground wires that attach to the cylinder head, just above the trans. Disconnect them, too.<BR>When you put it all back together, be sure to keep the wiring harness away from the exhaust system. <BR>You *will* give your most colorful vocabulary words a lot of exercise.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Good luck!<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||05-04-2000||12:06 PM||||Stupid me. I posted the thermostat answer on the wrong thread.<P>The hose you are/were referring to is the coolant crossover tube. GM no longer makes them, but Rodney Dickman has some excellent ones made from stainless steel for sale.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/</A> <p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 05-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||bad88||05-04-2000||12:24 PM||||Thanks for the info...a little steep for the pipe but since it's stainless and I wouldn't want to do this twice that seems like the way to go. Like I said in the las tnote it's in the shop now but maybe I can get them to install this pipe once I get it.||||reg||14
Z||000009||bad88||05-05-2000||11:32 PM||||o.k. problem resolved!!!!  It wasn't the coolent crossover pipe after all.  There is a second pipe that runs from the intake near the drivers side on the top (about 1/4" diameter) under the air intake hose from the filter.  I can't believe I missed but I was thinking that was some sort of vacuum line so I didn't even look there.  It seems that somehow the tube got worn through and then rusted the rest of the way creating a small enough hole to drain the fluid and cause the over heating.  The mechanic replaced it with brake line and a brass fitting and I'm back on the road for less than 60 bucks. (except for the 160 I spent for rushing the coolent tube from Rodney...oops) I have great things to say for Rodney though, He got the tube out next day at minimal cost and it looks sweet.  I would recommend anyone actually replacing this tube to forget repairs and get one from Rodney.  Even though I didn't install it I'm going to keep it until the OEM pipe finally rusts through  btw Tom, the mechanic says he probably would have used a few choice words had he needed to replace that pipe.  Thanks for all the great info everyone and especially thanks to Jeff for helping me install the water pump.  It's nice to know that when the chips are down there is somewhere to turn to keep the Fieros on the road<BR>Brian||||reg||10
Z||000010||GT Bastard||05-05-2000||11:49 PM||||Great news Brian!  I was glad to could help with the problem(just too bad the waterpump didn't fix it.)  It was a sorry sight to see a nice 88GT on jackstands!   Glad you're up and running.   ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||05-06-2000||08:44 PM||||Bad88, <BR>Way to go! Glad it wasn't the crossover. It's not a lot of fun.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
